  "result": "The gate returned **OK** for `drv.position.behind` on the third of six allowed runs (3 witnesses, 3 atoms read).\n\n**Body** (in `library/TrafficSpec/Concepts/position/behind.lean`): b counts as ahead of a when all three of these hold:\n- the headings are aligned within the `alignment` tolerance (`rel_same_direction`);\n- b's bearing from a points forward in a's frame (`rel_bearing`, \"ahead\");\n- the two bodies' lengths along a's heading don't overlap (`rel_longitudinal_overlap` below 0 m).\n\nIt reads no speed and no lane, as the contract requires. There are no narrowings, because the contract states every element exactly.\n\n**Witnesses:** `alignment` is set to 9/10. Both road users are cars heading east in two lanes.\n- **holds:** a moves along the left lane, well behind b, which is at rest in the right lane.\n- **fails:** a and b overlap by 2 m along a's heading. Bearing and alignment still hold there, so only the overlap decides the result.\n- **blind:** the holds scene with the overlap declared absent. Declaring the overlap absent isn't enough on its own, because Core recomputes it from the cars' positions and sizes. That was the first gate failure. So b's size (`kin.extent`) is also left unobserved. Without b's size the gate rejected the trace because b's occupancy was still marked observed (the second failure). I removed b from the observed occupancy in that scene.\n\nOne shell command was denied: a `grep` across Core that included a `cd`, which only the gate command is allowed to do. I read the same Core files with the Read tool instead, so nothing was lost.\n\nThe record had no defects. `task_result.md` is in the working directory and lists each gate run and the choices above.",
